// Two callers of `PageFault`, and the last of the four predictions this thread made about itself.
//
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` predicted `PageFault` (3) would earn its members because it has two kinds
// of caller, a renderer and a test harness. The rule that prediction came from has since been
// replaced — a decode union earns its members at the **structural/canonicality** boundary — and
// `PageFault` is not a decode union, so the new rule is silent about it and the old prediction is all
// there is. Writing it settles both.
//
// It lands in neither, and that is the result: **it needs a third mechanism, and naming it explains
// all four callers this thread has written.**

import { Result } from "core";
import { Page, PageFault, NoSuchElement, WrongSize, NotGranted } from "std";

/** Refuse, or carry on without the element. */
export enum Handling { Fatal, Ignore }

/**
 * A renderer patching a DOM it built.
 *
 * `NoSuchElement` means the renderer's own idea of the tree is wrong — it wrote that id — so it is
 * a bug in this program, not a condition. Everything here is `Fatal`, and the reason is that a
 * renderer's failures are all self-inflicted.
 */
export Handling forRenderer(PageFault f) {
  match (f) {
    NoSuchElement(_): { return Fatal; }
    WrongSize(_, _):  { return Fatal; }
    NotGranted:       { return Fatal; }
  }
}

/**
 * A test harness driving a page it did not build.
 *
 * `NoSuchElement` is the *ordinary* answer to *is the error banner showing* before it shows, so a
 * harness asking about an element that may not be there needs it as a value rather than a failure.
 */
export Handling forHarness(PageFault f) {
  match (f) {
    NoSuchElement(_): { return Ignore; }
    WrongSize(_, _):  { return Fatal; }
    NotGranted:       { return Fatal; }
  }
}

// ## 1. The prediction held and the reason was not the one given
//
// One row of three differs, so the union earns `NoSuchElement` as a separate member. But the two
// callers do not differ about *strictness*, and there is no recovered value for the lenient one to
// accept — `NoSuchElement` has nothing behind it. What they differ about is:
//
// > **whether the thing that failed is something this program wrote.**
//
// The renderer put that id in the tree; a missing element is a bug in the renderer. The harness was
// handed a page somebody else built; a missing element is a fact about the page. Same member, same
// payload, and the difference is which side of the boundary the *caller* sits on.
//
// So this is not the structural/canonicality boundary and it is not caller-counting. It is a third
// mechanism, and once named it accounts for the other three files without straining:
//
// | mechanism | the question the members answer | seen in |
// |---|---|---|
// | **recovery** | a value exists — hand it over or not? | `CodecFault`, `RlpFault`, `TimeFault` |
// | **response** | what outward artefact does this become? | `RequestFault` (a status), `UpdateFault` (a peer policy) |
// | **provenance** | did this program produce the thing that failed? | `PageFault` here, `FileFault` in `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac` |
//
// The third row is the one this thread kept finding and could not name. `cp`'s `Side` is exactly
// provenance — *is this fault about the operand I was handed or about the run I set up* — and it was
// passed by hand because the fault cannot know. `refuse.wac`'s constant `close` column is response
// with nothing to vary. `sync.wac`'s *whose fault* is provenance that happened to coincide with the
// member names.
//
// **And the three mechanisms differ in whether the union can carry the answer.** Recovery can: *is
// there a value* is a property of the member and nothing else. Response can: a status code is a fact
// about the fault. **Provenance cannot**, ever, because it is a fact about the call site — which is
// why three of the four callers in this thread had to supply their axis by hand and why the fourth
// only looked like it did not.
//
// That is the sharpest form this argument has reached, and it is falsifiable: a union whose callers
// differ on provenance will always need something the union cannot hold, and a union whose callers
// differ on recovery or response will not.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`forHarness` answering `Ignore` for `NoSuchElement` is asking a question with a failure.** *Is
// the error banner showing* is a question whose two answers are both normal, and it is spelled here
// as a call that fails. The type wants `Result<Element?, PageFault>` — absent is a value, and
// `PageFault` keeps the two that really are failures. Third instance of *normal but uninteresting is
// not a fault*, after `@/packages/lightclient`'s `NotRelevant` and `@/packages/datetime`'s
// `LeapSecond`, and the first where the fix is a `?` rather than a redesign.
//
// **`Page` has twelve slots and one `PageFault`**, which is the `Files` shape measured in
// `../../../std/platform.wac`: `WrongSize` is reachable from two of the twelve and dead in the other
// ten. The count there was 29 dead arms in 80; here it is smaller and the same, and both are the
// reason *share the members, vary the union* is written down and not done.
//
// **Provenance would be a property of the capability, not of the fault.** A `Page` this program
// built and a `Page` it was handed are the same type, and if they were not — if a renderer held a
// `Page<Mine>` and a harness a `Page<Theirs>` — then `NoSuchElement` would be a compile error for one
// of them rather than a policy. That is a phantom parameter, the ninth site for it in this directory,
// and the first where it would remove a fault member rather than prevent a mix-up.
